## Proof

**Claim:** Yes. A small part of a 2-torus can be locally isometrically embedded in affine $\mathbb{R}^3$.

**Setup.** The 2-torus $T^2 = S^1 \times S^1$ carries its standard flat Riemannian metric, obtained as the quotient of $(\mathbb{R}^2, g_{\text{Euc}})$ by the lattice $\mathbb{Z}^2$. The quotient map
$$\pi: \mathbb{R}^2 \to T^2 = \mathbb{R}^2/\mathbb{Z}^2$$
is a local isometry: every point $p \in T^2$ has a neighborhood $U$ such that $\pi^{-1}(U)$ is a disjoint union of open sets, each mapped isometrically onto $U$.

**Construction of the embedding.** Let $p \in T^2$ be any point. Choose $\tilde{p} \in \mathbb{R}^2$ with $\pi(\tilde{p}) = p$. Pick $\varepsilon < 1/4$ and let
$$V = B_\varepsilon(\tilde{p}) \subset \mathbb{R}^2, \qquad U = \pi(V) \subset T^2.$$
Since $\varepsilon < 1/4$, the restriction $\pi|_V : V \to U$ is an isometry (it is injective and a local isometry). Now define
$$\iota: V \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}^3, \qquad (x,y) \mapsto (x, y, 0).$$
This is the standard inclusion of the $xy$-plane, which is an isometric embedding of $(V, g_{\text{Euc}})$ into $(\mathbb{R}^3, g_{\text{Euc}})$: the induced metric on the image is $dx^2 + dy^2$, matching the Euclidean metric on $V$.

Composing, we obtain the map
$$f = \iota \circ (\pi|_V)^{-1} : U \to \mathbb{R}^3,$$
which is a smooth embedding (being a composition of a diffeomorphism and an embedding). Since both $\pi|_V$ and $\iota$ are isometries onto their images, $f$ is an **isometric embedding** of the Riemannian manifold $(U, g|_U)$ into $\mathbb{R}^3$.

**Verification of the isometry condition.** For any tangent vectors $v, w \in T_q U$ at $q \in U$, we have:
$$\langle df_q(v), df_q(w) \rangle_{\mathbb{R}^3} = \langle d\iota_{\tilde{q}} \circ d(\pi|_V)^{-1}_q(v),\; d\iota_{\tilde{q}} \circ d(\pi|_V)^{-1}_q(w) \rangle_{\mathbb{R}^3} = \langle d(\pi|_V)^{-1}_q(v),\; d(\pi|_V)^{-1}_q(w) \rangle_{\mathbb{R}^2} = \langle v, w \rangle_{g_q},$$
where the last equality uses that $\pi|_V$ is an isometry. Thus $f^* g_{\mathbb{R}^3} = g|_U$, confirming the isometric embedding.

**Remark (generality).** This result is also a special case of the **Janet–Cartan theorem** (1926–1927): every real-analytic $n$-dimensional Riemannian manifold admits a local isometric embedding into $\mathbb{R}^{n(n+1)/2}$. For $n = 2$, the target is $\mathbb{R}^3$. Since the flat torus is real-analytic, the theorem guarantees the existence of such an embedding for any small neighborhood. In the flat case, the explicit construction above (embedding as a planar patch) is the simplest realization.

**Conclusion.** A small open neighborhood of any point on the flat 2-torus $T^2$ can be locally isometrically embedded in $\mathbb{R}^3$, for instance as a flat patch in the $xy$-plane.

